WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:01.777
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Are you looking

00:00:01.777 --> 00:00:03.195
to optimize
application capacity?

00:00:03.195 --> 00:00:05.070
STEPHANIE WONG: Amongst
all the other things,

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:07.530
global load balancing is
definitely one to check out.

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:08.988
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Well, stay tuned

00:00:08.988 --> 00:00:12.232
to learn more in this episode
of "Get Cooking In Cloud."

00:00:12.232 --> 00:00:16.200
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:23.170
Last time, we talked
about load balancing

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:27.190
on GCP, including HTTPS
and network load balancing.

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:29.710
There are a lot of ways
to setup a load balancer

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:32.170
and back end instances
to support something

00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:34.945
like Beyond Treat's
growing e-commerce website.

00:00:34.945 --> 00:00:36.820
STEPHANIE WONG: They've
become a national hit

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:38.740
and need to start scaling
their website back

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:40.330
ends to meet demand.

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:42.700
One thing to highlight
is GCP's compute engine

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:45.820
manage instance groups to
take advantage of auto scaling

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:46.960
capabilities.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:49.000
This lets you
automatically add or delete

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.070
instances from a
managed instance group

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:53.458
based on increases
or decreases in load.

00:00:53.458 --> 00:00:55.750
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Auto
scaling helps your applications

00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:57.940
gracefully handle
increase in traffic

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:01.240
and reduce cost when the
need for resources is lower.

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:03.130
You define the
auto scaling policy

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:05.620
and the auto scaler
performs automatic scaling

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:07.560
based on the measured load.

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:09.370
STEPHANIE WONG: Check
out episodes 4 and 5

00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:12.297
from season one of this series
for more on auto scaling.

00:01:12.297 --> 00:01:14.380
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: But
scaling global applications

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:16.150
can be challenging,
especially if you

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:18.910
have limited IT budgets and
unpredictable and burst-y

00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:19.900
workloads.

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:21.460
You have holidays
in each country

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:25.150
that spikes demand for treat
orders at different times.

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:27.340
On Google Cloud, the
flexibility provided

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:30.860
by features like auto scaling
and load balancing can help.

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:33.288
However, auto scalers
have some limitations.

00:01:33.288 --> 00:01:35.080
STEPHANIE WONG: There
are five major issues

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:36.880
that come up with auto scalers.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:38.770
The most common issue
is that your auto

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:41.110
scaled new back end
running your website

00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:43.162
isn't ready to serve
your traffic fast enough.

00:01:43.162 --> 00:01:44.620
You might have
startup scripts that

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:47.390
need to be loaded before
VM instances are ready,

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:49.630
meaning traffic ends up
at existing instances

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:50.975
that are over capacity.

00:01:50.975 --> 00:01:53.350
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Second
issue is that some applications

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:55.130
cannot be auto scaled at all.

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:59.470
For example, databases often
have limited back end capacity.

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:01.440
Only a specific
number of front ends

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:04.293
can access a database that
doesn't scale horizontally.

00:02:04.293 --> 00:02:05.710
STEPHANIE WONG:
The third issue is

00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:08.289
that your VM back ends might
have too little headroom

00:02:08.289 --> 00:02:09.370
capacity.

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:11.110
To account for a sudden
burst of traffic,

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:14.870
an auto scaler should include
ample headroom for example,

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:16.990
you can set the auto
scaler to go into effect

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:20.710
when your back end reaches
70% CPU utilization.

00:02:20.710 --> 00:02:22.870
To save costs, you
might be tempted

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:25.570
to set this target
higher, like 90%.

00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:28.060
But this can result
in scaling bottlenecks

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:29.740
when confronted with
bursts of traffic,

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:33.100
like an advertising campaign
that suddenly increases demand.

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:35.380
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Fourth
issue is regional coders.

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:38.140
If you have unexpected
bursts in a region,

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:40.630
your resource coders
in that region

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:43.132
might limit the number of
instances you can scale to.

00:02:43.132 --> 00:02:44.590
STEPHANIE WONG:
And the fifth issue

00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:47.620
is that you might have
non-elastic licenses.

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:49.720
When you use licensed
software, you

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:52.090
might have a preset
maximum capacity.

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:54.010
So you might not be
able to auto scale

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:57.075
and run that software on more
than let's say 5 instances.

00:02:57.075 --> 00:02:59.200
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: You can
address these challenges

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:01.750
with GCP's global load
balancing solutions,

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:06.880
like HTTPS load balancing, SSL,
and TCP proxy load balancing.

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:08.620
First, let's walk
through a recipe

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:12.208
of how our global load balancers
handle bursts in traffic.

00:03:12.208 --> 00:03:14.500
STEPHANIE WONG: As you may
know, typical load balancers

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:17.110
use an algorithmic approach
to distribute traffic

00:03:17.110 --> 00:03:18.100
to back end servers.

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:22.060
Like round robin, where packets
are equally distributed between

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:25.418
all back ends regardless of the
packet source and destination.

00:03:25.418 --> 00:03:27.460
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Or
hashing, where packet flows

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:30.850
are identified based on
hashes of traffic information,

00:03:30.850 --> 00:03:35.050
including source IP, destination
IP, port, and protocol.

00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:38.110
All traffic that produces
the same hash value

00:03:38.110 --> 00:03:39.790
flows to the same back end.

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:41.207
STEPHANIE WONG:
Today, we're going

00:03:41.207 --> 00:03:43.480
to focus on Google's
global HTTPS load balancer

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:46.930
for layer 4 HTTP
or HTTPS traffic.

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:50.590
Typical HTTPS load balancers
use a DNS-based algorithm

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:53.590
serving to different load
balancers in each region.

00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:56.060
Failover to another
region is possible,

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:58.030
but it can take
minutes based on time

00:03:58.030 --> 00:04:00.550
to live or TTL configurations.

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:02.380
Generally, a small
amount of traffic

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:05.530
is still directed to old
servers well past the TTL.

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:08.020
This makes it not ideal
for burst-y loads.

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:10.010
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
GCP's global HTTPS

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:12.430
a load balancer uses
a different approach.

00:04:12.430 --> 00:04:16.180
Traffic is proxied through the
Google front end or GFE servers

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:19.810
deployed throughout most of the
global network edge locations.

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:23.360
Then the load balancer algorithm
is applied at GFE servers.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:24.860
STEPHANIE WONG:
Here's how it works.

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:28.150
The HTTPS load balancer
uses a special algorithm

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:30.010
called waterfall by region.

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:32.260
Traffic that goes through
the load balancer IP address

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:35.290
is sent through a proxy
to the back end instances.

00:04:35.290 --> 00:04:37.330
The algorithm determines
an optimal back end

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:40.000
by taking into account the
proximity of the instances

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:43.600
to the users, the incoming
load, and the available capacity

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:46.000
of the back ends in
each region and zone.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:48.190
The load balancer then
distributes traffic

00:04:48.190 --> 00:04:50.140
based on available instances.

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:52.083
To add new instances
based on the load,

00:04:52.083 --> 00:04:53.500
the algorithm works
in conjunction

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:55.493
with auto scaling
instance groups.

00:04:55.493 --> 00:04:56.410
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: OK.

00:04:56.410 --> 00:04:57.910
Let's walk through
some scenarios

00:04:57.910 --> 00:05:00.430
when Beyond Treat's
traffic load becomes

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:02.140
too much for its
managed instance

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:04.270
group to handle in one region.

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:07.570
First, let's talk about the
traffic flow in one region.

00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:10.120
Under normal
circumstances, all traffic

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:12.950
is sent to the region
closest to the user.

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:14.680
Then the following happens.

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:17.450
Traffic is distributed
across instance groups,

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:19.700
which can be in multiple zones.

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:22.140
If capacity is
unequal between zones,

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:25.350
zones are loaded in proportion
to their available serving

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:26.370
capacity.

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:28.980
Within zones, requests
are spread evenly

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:31.950
over the instances in
each instance group.

00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:34.290
STEPHANIE WONG: If an entire
region reaches capacity

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:37.590
because the capacity limits you
assigned to an instance group,

00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:40.260
the waterfall by region
algorithm is triggered

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:42.900
and traffic overflows to
the next closest region that

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:44.730
has available capacity.

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:47.040
As each region reaches
capacity, traffic

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:50.395
spills over to the next
closest region, and so on.

00:05:50.395 --> 00:05:52.770
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Let's say
you have unhealthy back ends

00:05:52.770 --> 00:05:55.380
and you need cross
regional overflow.

00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:57.450
If the health check
sees more than half

00:05:57.450 --> 00:05:59.820
of the back ends in the
region are unhealthy,

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:03.060
the GFE servers preemptively
overflows some traffic

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:05.010
to the next closest region.

00:06:05.010 --> 00:06:07.680
That way you don't have
an entire region failing

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:09.318
as the region becomes unhealthy.

00:06:09.318 --> 00:06:10.110
STEPHANIE WONG: OK.

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:12.990
Now let's say all the regions
you have instance groups in

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:14.850
are above capacity.

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:16.890
Traffic is then
balanced so every region

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:19.140
is at the same relative
level of overflow

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:20.910
compared to its capacity.

00:06:20.910 --> 00:06:25.680
For example, if global demand
exceeds global capacity by 20%,

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:28.455
traffic is distributed so that
all regions receive requests

00:06:28.455 --> 00:06:31.860
at 20% over regional capacity
while keeping traffic

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:33.382
as local as possible.

00:06:33.382 --> 00:06:35.340
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So
how does using HTTPS load

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:38.370
balancing actually address
the five capacity challenges

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:39.820
we talked about
in the beginning.

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:41.070
STEPHANIE WONG: Let's see how.

00:06:41.070 --> 00:06:43.920
Now that we've discussed how
the GFE and waterfall by region

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:49.020
algorithm works, the HTTPS TCP
proxy and the SSL proxy load

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:52.560
balancers can overflow
capacity to other regions.

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:55.140
This means that it's a good
idea to use a global load

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:58.380
balancer over the regional load
balancers, especially when it

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:01.290
comes to global e-commerce
sites like Beyond Treat.

00:07:01.290 --> 00:07:04.770
Apps that use regional back ends
have nominally lower latency,

00:07:04.770 --> 00:07:07.400
but they can become
overloaded very easily.

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:10.140
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: For latency
in starting new instances,

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:14.220
if the auto scaler cannot add
capacity fast enough during

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:18.030
bursts, the HTTPS load balancer
will temporarily overflow

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:20.490
connections to the
next closest region.

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:22.620
As soon as back end
instances are scaled up

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:24.750
in the original
region, new traffic

00:07:24.750 --> 00:07:27.392
is routed to region
closest to the users again.

00:07:27.392 --> 00:07:29.850
STEPHANIE WONG: For apps like
databases limited by back end

00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:32.040
capacity, you can
still overflow traffic

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:34.410
to the next closest region
when demand in one region

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:37.408
is beyond capacity deployed
for usual traffic needs.

00:07:37.408 --> 00:07:39.450
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: As for
too little VM headroom,

00:07:39.450 --> 00:07:41.820
regional overflow
still saves money

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:45.510
because you can either setup
auto scaling with a high CPU

00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:49.500
usage trigger, or make back end
capacity well under a region's

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:51.990
capacity since overflow
to other regions

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:55.453
ensure global capacity
will always be sufficient.

00:07:55.453 --> 00:07:57.120
STEPHANIE WONG: Now
for regional quotas,

00:07:57.120 --> 00:07:59.820
if compute engine resource
quotas don't match demand,

00:07:59.820 --> 00:08:02.130
load balancing overflow
automatically redirects

00:08:02.130 --> 00:08:04.800
part of the traffic to a
region that can still scale

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:06.218
within its regional quota.

00:08:06.218 --> 00:08:08.010
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: In
the case of licenses,

00:08:08.010 --> 00:08:10.770
the load balancer can
move traffic to a region

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:12.900
where licenses are available.

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:16.170
You just set the maximum
number of instances to the same

00:08:16.170 --> 00:08:18.678
as the maximum number of
licenses on the auto scaler.

00:08:18.678 --> 00:08:20.220
STEPHANIE WONG: It's
key to point out

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:22.020
that you do need a set
up and manage instance

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:24.520
groups in the regions you feel
are appropriate for your user

00:08:24.520 --> 00:08:25.260
base.

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:26.927
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
You have to also take

00:08:26.927 --> 00:08:29.280
into account things like app
licenses and dependencies

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:31.950
like database scaling to
meet the demand of auto

00:08:31.950 --> 00:08:32.970
scaled website servers.

00:08:32.970 --> 00:08:34.470
STEPHANIE WONG: For
more information

00:08:34.470 --> 00:08:36.370
about GCP's global
load balancing,

00:08:36.370 --> 00:08:39.289
check out the solution article
by our friend, [INAUDIBLE]..

00:08:39.289 --> 00:08:39.789
Link below.

00:08:39.789 --> 00:08:41.414
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
That's all for today

00:08:41.414 --> 00:08:42.659
on Get Cooking In Cloud.

00:08:42.659 --> 00:08:44.820
Hopefully we were
able to intrigue you

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:47.220
with ingredients and
recipe to whip up

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:51.300
a resilient and performing
dessert site for our global fan

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:51.870
base.

00:08:51.870 --> 00:08:52.920
STEPHANIE WONG: If
you liked this video

00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:54.910
and would like to see
